---
// The privacy page, at /privacy: what the hosted playmat collects and keeps,
// for how long, and what it never collects. Each statement follows the
// playmat as built (internal/hostauth, internal/usergames, internal/game);
// the idle time comes from the App Platform definition, not typed in.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { limits, site } from '../lib/facts';

const updated = '8 November 2026';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What the hosted tcgreplay playmat collects when you sign in and play, how long it keeps it, and what it never collects."
>
  <article class="privacy">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}.</p>
    <p>
      This page covers the hosted playmat at <a href={site.publicURL}>{site.publicURL.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
      If you run tcgreplay on your own computer, nothing leaves your machine except the download of the project's
      published card data and the card images your browser loads. This website itself sets no cookies and loads
      nothing from other sites.
    </p>

    <section id="sign-in" aria-labelledby="h-sign-in">
      <h2 id="h-sign-in">When you sign in</h2>
      <p>
        You sign in with Google. The playmat asks Google only for your email address (the <code>openid</code> and
        <code>email</code> permissions) and keeps three things:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your Google account ID, a number Google uses to tell accounts apart;</li>
        <li>your email address;</li>
        <li>whether Google has verified that email address.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        It never asks for or keeps your name, photo or password. Google hands the playmat a token when you sign in,
        which it uses once to confirm who you are and then throws away.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your sign-ins are kept encrypted, in the playmat's memory only. Two cookies keep you signed in, both hidden
        from scripts:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>tcgreplay_session</code>, which keeps the board signed in;</li>
        <li><code>tcgreplay_login_…</code>, which exists only while a Google sign-in is in progress.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The board also remembers four display settings in your browser (replay speed, autoplay, where the panel sits and
        prize reminders). They never leave your browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="while-playing" aria-labelledby="h-while-playing">
      <h2 id="h-while-playing">While you play</h2>
      <p>
        The playmat keeps your current game: the battle log and decklists you import, the moves on the board, its
        branches, the assistant's coaching notes and the opponent's likely list. Each Google account has its own game,
        and only that account can open its board.
      </p>
      <p>
        All of it is held in memory only. It is never written to disk or to any storage service, and it is gone when
        the playmat restarts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="how-long" aria-labelledby="h-how-long">
      <h2 id="h-how-long">How long it is kept</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your game</strong> is dropped after <span data-idle>{limits.idleTimeout}</span> without activity, when
          your last sign-in ends, and whenever the playmat is updated or restarted. To keep a game, ask your assistant for
          its record and save it yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your account details</strong> (ID, email, verified flag) are kept with your sign-ins and dropped
          when your last one ends: when you sign out and disconnect your assistant, or when they run out (below).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Board sign-in</strong> lasts up to 30 days. <strong>Your assistant's connection</strong> lasts up to
          30 days and is renewed while you use it; each access it is given lasts one hour. A Google sign-in in progress
          lasts up to 10 minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Logs.</strong> The playmat writes your email address to its logs when you sign in or out, when a limit
          stops a request, and when your game expires or is removed. Logs never contain IP addresses, battle logs or
          decks. The hosting providers, DigitalOcean and Cloudflare, may keep their own request logs.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="outside-services" aria-labelledby="h-outside-services">
      <h2 id="h-outside-services">Outside services</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Google</strong>, for signing in.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>TCGdex</strong> and <strong>malie.io</strong>, the sources of the card data, which the project builds
          and publishes ahead of time; the playmat reads only that published copy, so nothing about you is sent to
          them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Card images</strong> are loaded by your browser, from the playmat's own image store, TCGdex or
          malie.io.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>DigitalOcean</strong> hosts the playmat, and <strong>Cloudflare</strong> delivers its traffic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your AI assistant</strong> (Claude, ChatGPT or another) sees what you share with it and what the
          playmat tells it, under that assistant's own privacy terms.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="never" aria-labelledby="h-never">
      <h2 id="h-never">What it never collects</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your password, name or photo.</li>
        <li>Your Google access tokens, after the moment of sign-in.</li>
        <li>Analytics, tracking or advertising data of any kind.</li>
        <li>Anything to sell or share: the playmat sells or shares nothing.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="removing" aria-labelledby="h-removing">
      <h2 id="h-removing">Removing your data</h2>
      <p>
        Sign out from the board's menu, and disconnect tcgreplay in your assistant's connector settings. There is no
        stored account to delete: once your sign-ins end, the playmat forgets you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="h-contact">
      <h2 id="h-contact">Questions</h2>
      <p>Open an issue on <a href={`${site.repoURL}/issues`}>the project's GitHub page</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy { max-width: 46rem; }
  .updated { color: var(--muted); }
  section { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  li { margin: 0.3rem 0; }
</style>
